# Code style — arrows & naming

## Arrow functions

Use ES6 arrow functions. Do not use `function` declarations or `function` expressions.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
export async function activate(...) { ... }
function readArtboard(id: string): Artboard | undefined { ... }

// ✅ GOOD
export const activate = async (...) => { ... }
const readArtboard = (artboardId: string): Artboard | undefined => { ... }
```

Exceptions: class methods / `constructor` (classes need them). VS Code extension entry may keep the shape the API expects.

## Naming — one look must tell you what it is

No one-letter or cryptic names. Prefer full words that name the thing.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
catch (err) { ... }
const opts = ...
const res = await ...
const ab = readArtboard(id)
webview.postMessage({ t: 'u', d })

// ✅ GOOD
catch (error) { ... }
const options = ...
const response = await ...
const artboard = readArtboard(artboardId)
webview.postMessage({ type: 'artboardUpdated', payload })
```

### Rules of thumb

- Errors: `error`, never `err` / `e`
- Async I/O: `response` / `payload` / `requestText`, not `res` / `raw`
- Domain abbreviations only when the full name is already in the identifier (`artboardId`, not `abId`)
- Wire/protocol field names stay as the contract defines them (MCP tool args, disk JSON keys, webview message `type`)

When renaming, update all call sites in the same change.

## Single source of truth

Disk layout, artboard schema version, and MCP tool names live in one module each and are imported. Do not re-type path segments under `.cursor-design/` or tool name strings across host / mcp / webview.

## File size

A module that mixes MCP handlers, disk SoT, and webview HTML into hundreds of lines should be split by concern. Keep call sites importing the same public helpers. Do not split a 150-line file that does one thing.

## Code comments

- If you are writing comments they should be to the point. Explaining why was something done over what the code does.

## TypeScript

- Explicit return types on exported helpers, MCP tool handlers, and disk readers/writers.
- Do **not** add `: JSX.Element` / `: React.JSX.Element` on every Svelte/component wrapper.
- Reusable types only if used in 2+ places or they're a real domain type. No `types.ts` dump — put the type next to the owner.

## Object arguments (3+)

Utils / helpers with **3 or more** parameters take one object. Named keys catch omitted flags and swapped ids; positional lists do not.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
export const writeArtboard = (
  artboardId: string,
  html: string,
  generation: number,
  workspaceRoot: string,
) => { ... }

writeArtboard(id, html, generation) // workspaceRoot silently dropped

// ✅ GOOD
type WriteArtboardArgs = {
  artboardId: string
  html: string
  generation: number
  workspaceRoot: string
}

export const writeArtboard = ({
  artboardId,
  html,
  generation,
  workspaceRoot,
}: WriteArtboardArgs) => { ... }
```

Exceptions:

- Ordered coordinates / indices
- Stdlib-shaped callbacks `(value, index)`
- Platform-shaped APIs: `fs.readFile(path, encoding)`, `webview.postMessage(message)`, VS Code command handlers
- Local test factories in `*.test.ts`
